    },
    "body": "After the pointer arm first emitted a recalled value, the expert review recommended extending that run rather than redesigning it, and I repeated that recommendation twice. THE MATCHED CONTROL REFUTES IT FOR THIS AXIS: a control arm ran the identical fine-tune as the identity arms - same checkpoint, mixture, recipe and 12,207 steps - with only the identity term omitted, and scored 24/84 = 0.2857 against the un-fine-tuned baseline 35/84 = 0.4167. So additional fine-tuning ALONE costs about 13 points of recall, and the identity arms' larger losses are that cost plus the term's own. WHY IT IS A RETRACTION: the advice assumed the selection curve tracked what matters, but the session had already shown three times that dev_pointer_hit_rate does not - it sits at the baseline's level for an arm whose exact copies fell to 10/84, and is frozen to all printed digits for an arm that cannot copy at all. WHAT CHANGES: the best measured binding state is the ORIGINAL arm-ptr checkpoint, not a longer run; future arms must use a MATCHED fine-tune control, not just the starting checkpoint; the failure is localised to the GATE (span mass 0.8399 vs the control's 0.7415, gate 0.1163 vs 0.2439, emitted probability 0.1300 vs 0.2292); so the remaining lever is a change that keeps the gate high while preserving span attention - neither more training nor more identity. References #820",
